  "full_text": "So military conditioning is what I called it when you become, you have some things like an ability to take on a psychopathic attitude and it brings comfort to get your job done. It's a professionalism, the ability to be a psychopath is what would normally be considered psychopathic. All the things in the military, you don't cry over spilled milk, you know. On some level, it's becoming, well, less human, more machine, I guess is how they put it. Anyway, so there's actually... This is good, but it's bad if you use it in the wrong place. So, like, you know... You shouldn't be less human when it's not required to any machine when it's not required. You know, for example, begging. Begging, you know. You know there's something hateful about begging. But in certain psychopathic outlooks, It's not hateful. And then you can tilt the pinball machine or try to, and it never works. You know, like, oh, well, there's no downside in begging. But that's a strong hint. You're probably being a little bit, I don't know, you just, begging is a, you can get the notion there's no downside, you know. And that's not how you do this psychopathic begging. The same is true of spilt milk. You can get the impression that, well, I don't cry over spilt milk. And there's something wrong with this. So I experienced this with crying when a match went out, you know. And then I started lighting them off and we're getting, oh, something's wrong here, you know. So matches are primal, primal. You've got to grok the match. And it's more, I was thinking of it kind of like a fluffy sparrow. Sparrows look fluffy, I don't know how to put it, but there's a beauty there. And a human lighting a match is, I don't know, that was what came to mind because I was thinking about them recently.",
